<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sava</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sava River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sava is a river of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, about 950 kilometres long, which rises in the Julian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a> and flows east through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and along the Bosnian border to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>. Its valley was a major route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, guarded in Roman times by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisak">Siscia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a>. For centuries it marked frontiers between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and the Balkan states and later between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> and Ottoman empires.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sava.jpg" alt="Sava" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petar Milošević · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sava_River_and_Gazela_Bridge_(Belgrade).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
